cli.mcp.link:
Sign-in to {server}: this hub has no browser — send the link to a person.
{url}
They sign in on their side; the browser will then show an error "cannot open localhost" — that is expected: they should send the address from the browser's address bar (http://localhost:…/callback?code=…). Send it here within ~5 minutes: hub mcp login {name} {server} --redirect '<address>'. If it was too late, the same command without --redirect gives a new link.

cli.mcp.old_link:
Sign-in to {server}: this hub has no browser — send the link to the person.
{url}
They sign in on their side; the browser then shows the error "cannot open localhost" — that is expected: let them send the address from the browser bar (http://localhost:…/callback?code=…). Put it here within ~5 minutes: hub mcp login {name} {server} --redirect '<address>'. Too late — the same command without --redirect gives a new link.

cli.secrets.usage:
`hub secret list` — the secrets the owner keeps in the hub: names, sites, whether commands may have them (no values);
`hub secret add <name> --from-file <file|-> [--key KEY] [--login-key KEY | --login TEXT] [--site S]… [--commands] [--note "…"]` — put a value from a file the owner pointed you to (.env, an export; `-` — stdin) into the vault without seeing it; an existing name gets the new value, its sites and switch stay the owner's;
`hub secret fill <name> [password|username]` — put one into the form field where your cursor is, on one of its sites;
`hub secret exec --env VAR=<name>[:login] … -- <command> [args]` — run a command with the value in its environment (only secrets the owner allowed into commands); the value is masked in the command's output.

You never get the value: never ask the owner for it, never print it, never open the file you add from. The mask catches printing by accident, not on purpose (base64, a file). Prefer programs that read the variable themselves (GH_TOKEN, GITLAB_TOKEN…) or `curl -H @-` over putting it into arguments: on Linux every user of the machine sees them.

