core.update.edits_on_red_dev:
The owner pressed «Put the edits back»: the merge of commit {commit} of the backup branch {branch} into the hub's code went through, but the core tests are red on it (checked in a separate copy — the hub's code is untouched). Failed: {names}. The end of the output:
{tail}
Find what breaks (usually an own edit against the new code), fix it in a commit on top of git merge {commit}, run the tests, then hub send hub "restart the whole hub: put the own edits of the core back". The report goes to the owner.

core.update.lost_dev:
The release the hub's edits are counted from moved ({old} → {new}). In files the hub edits that guard sessions, access, data, the update or the journal, {count} hunk(s) of the release have added lines that are not in the merged file — check whether the merge lost them:
{hunks}
The check is coarse (most such signals are rewrites, not losses). For each: compare `git diff {old} {new} -- <file>` with the file now; nothing lost — hub update lost ok <id>; lost — put the change back with a commit, tests, then hub update lost restored <id>. The owner sees each open one as a line in Settings → «Own edits of the core».

core.update.red_dev:
The update ({label}, {new} commits) did not go in: the tests are red after the merge (checked in a separate copy — the hub's code is untouched). The end of the output:
{tail}
{failed}Find what breaks (often a personal edit against the new code), fix it and hub update.

core.update.shadow_red_dev:
The update to {version} is installed, but the release's versions of the tests this hub edits go red on the hub's code: the hub's edit changes what the release checks. Test files: {files}. Failed: {names}. The end of the output:
{tail}
The update is not held (for now this is a line in Settings → «Own edits of the core»). Do not change the release's test to make it pass. Fix the own edit so it keeps what the release checks; if the edit changes that behaviour on purpose, tell the owner of the hub what and why. The next update checks it again.

